@meta
id: 09-13
title: Yank a block with the bracket motion
chapter: 09
type: lesson
skills: op-motion
par: 36
minutes: 5
vscode: select five lines by eye, copy, paste, fix the name
open: queues.ts

@brief
The refund queue needs the same settings as the order queue. Its
declaration is there, waiting for a value:

    export const refundQueue = ;

Give it an object identical to `orderQueue`'s except that `name` is
`'refunds'`. Do not retype the object — it is four lines and every one
of them is already in the file.

@keys
| `%` | jump to the matching bracket, brace or paren |
| `y%` | yank from the cursor to the matching bracket, inclusive |
| `d%` | delete a bracketed block the same way |
| `f{` | forward onto the opening brace |

@hints
`%` is a motion, and a very precise one: from a brace it finds its
partner however many lines away that is. Any operator will take it.
--
With the cursor on `{`, `y%` copies the brace, everything inside it, and
the closing brace. Then put it where the missing value goes.
--
`f{` then `y%`. `G` to the last line, `$` to the semicolon, `h` to the
space before it, then `p`. Then `j0`, `f'`, `l` and `cw` to fix
the name in the copy.

@solution
    f{ y% G $ h p    j 0 f' l cw refunds <Esc>    :wq<Enter>

`y%` is the cheapest way to copy a block whose end you cannot see. You do
not count lines, you do not select, you do not scroll to check where the
brace closes — `%` already knows, because it is matching brackets rather
than guessing.

`p` after this yank puts the copied text after the cursor, and because the
copy spans several lines the result spills onto new lines by itself. That
is why landing on the space before the semicolon matters: put it after the
space and the object starts where the value should start, and the
semicolon ends up neatly after the closing brace.

The same motion with `d` in front deletes a whole call's arguments, a
whole object or a whole block: `d%` from the `(` of a function's
parameter list clears it in three keystrokes. Chapter 10 gives you text
objects, which are better still when the cursor is *inside* the block —
but `%` works from the outside, and it is the only one of the two that
also works as a plain jump.

@start queues.ts
export const orderQueue = {
  name: 'orders',
  retries: 3,
  backoffMs: 500,
};

export const refundQueue = ;

@expected queues.ts
export const orderQueue = {
  name: 'orders',
  retries: 3,
  backoffMs: 500,
};

export const refundQueue = {
  name: 'refunds',
  retries: 3,
  backoffMs: 500,
};
